<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-III-level_curves" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Level Curves</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this section, we will explore level curves of functions of two variables. 
    A <term>level curve</term> of a function <m>f(x, y)</m> is the set of all points <m>(x, y)</m> 
    in the domain of <m>f</m> such that <m>f(x, y) = k</m> for some constant <m>k</m>. 
    Level curves are useful for visualizing the behavior of a function in two dimensions.
  </p>
      <image source="level_curve.png" width="30%"/>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the level curves of the function <m>f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2</m> for <m>k = 1, 4, 9</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      For a given constant <m>k</m>, the level curve is defined by:
      <md>
        x^2 + y^2 = k
      </md>
      This represents a circle centered at the origin with radius <m>\sqrt{k}</m>. Therefore:
      <ul>
        <li>For <m>k = 1</m>, the level curve is a circle with radius 1.</li>
        <li>For <m>k = 4</m>, the level curve is a circle with radius 2.</li>
        <li>For <m>k = 9</m>, the level curve is a circle with radius 3.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the level curves of the function <m>f(x, y) = x^2 - y^2</m> for <m>k = 1, 0, -1</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      For a given constant <m>k</m>, the level curve is defined by:
      <md>
        x^2 - y^2 = k
      </md>
      This represents a hyperbola. Therefore:
      <ul>
        <li>For <m>k = 1</m>, the level curve is a hyperbola opening along the x-axis.</li>
        <li>For <m>k = 0</m>, the level curve is the pair of lines <m>y = \pm x</m>.</li>
        <li>For <m>k = -1</m>, the level curve is a hyperbola opening along the y-axis.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Sketch the level curves of the function <m>f(x, y) = \sqrt{9 - x^2 - y^2}</m> for <m>k = 0, 1, 2, 3</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
     <image source="level_curve_2.png" width="30%"/>
    <p>
      For a given constant <m>k</m>, the level curve is defined by:
      <md>
        \sqrt{9 - x^2 - y^2} = k \implies 9 - x^2 - y^2 = k^2
      </md>
      This represents a circle centered at the origin with radius <m>\sqrt{9 - k^2}</m>. Therefore:
      <ul>
        <li>For <m>k = 0</m>, the level curve is a circle with radius 3.</li>
        <li>For <m>k = 1</m>, the level curve is a circle with radius \sqrt{8}.</li>
        <li>For <m>k = 2</m>, the level curve is a circle with radius \sqrt{5}.</li>
        <li>For <m>k = 3</m>, the level curve is a circle with radius 0.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </solution> 
</example>
</introduction>




</section>